<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tirana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tirana is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, in the centre of the country. Traditionally founded as a town by the Ottoman notable Sulejman Pasha in 1614, it was chosen as the capital by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-lushnje">Congress of Lushnjë</a> in 1920. It grew under Zog, Italian rule and the communist regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a>, and since 1991 it has become the country's economic and cultural centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tirana.jpg" alt="Tirana" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo: Chris Walts · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skanderbeg_square_tirana_2016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tirana lies on the Plain of Tirana in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, between Mount Dajti to the east, hills to the south and a valley to the north that overlooks the Adriatic Sea. Its average altitude is about 110 metres. The Tiranë and Lanë rivers flow through the city. Dajti National Park lies on the mountain above it, with forests of pine, oak and beech, and the city's mountains are a popular retreat in winter, when snow often covers them although the city itself rarely receives it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been inhabited since prehistoric times, with the earliest recognised reference at the Cave of Pëllumbas, of the Palaeolithic period. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a> were its first population and most likely formed the core of the Illyrian kingdom of the Taulantii. After the Illyrian Wars the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> integrated the area into their empire. The oldest surviving find from that period is a Roman house of the 3rd century, later turned into a church with a mosaic floor. A castle, possibly known as Tirkan, was built by the Byzantine Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> and was restored by Ahmed Pasha Toptani in the 18th century.</p><p>Tirana appears in Venetian documents of the 1350s and in 1418. The first Ottoman land registrations of 1431–32 record 60 inhabited areas with nearly 2,028 houses and 7,300 inhabitants. In 1510 Marin Barleti, in his biography of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a>, called it a small village, distinguishing Little Tirana from Great Tirana. The town was founded in 1614 by the Ottoman Albanian nobleman Sylejman Pasha Bargjini, who built the Old Mosque, a small commercial centre and a hammam. Some sources describe a bazaar with watermills already existing before 1614. In the 16th century craftsmen made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> fabrics, leather, ceramics and metal artefacts. The Et'hem Bey Mosque was completed in 1821.</p><p>The city was fairly unimportant until the 20th century, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-lushnje">Congress of Lushnjë</a> proclaimed it the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> following the Albanian Declaration of Independence of 1912. Its present appearance was shaped by two totalitarian regimes: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>'s, whose architects Florestano Di Fausto and Armando Brasini designed the ministry buildings, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a>'s afterwards. The population tenfolded between 1820 and 1955, and it grew faster after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tirana is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> and seat of its government, parliament and highest courts. The centre from the University of Tirana's Mother Teresa Square to Skanderbeg Square was declared a protected cultural area in 2000. The 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 598,176 residents in the municipality. The airport is named after Mother Teresa.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tirana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Tirana</b> remained the capital, and the strong presidential powers of 1925 passed to the crown.</blockquote><a href="/state/albanian-kingdom">Albanian Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>A constituent assembly summoned in <b>Tirana</b> offered the crown to Victor Emmanuel III, creating a personal union with Italy.</blockquote><a href="/state/albanian-kingdom-1939-1943">Albanian Kingdom (1939-1943)</a></li><li><blockquote>Enver Hoxha's Partisans won the civil war and took <b>Tirana</b> on 17 November 1944 as German forces withdrew.</blockquote><a href="/state/albanian-kingdom-1943-1944">Albanian Kingdom (1943-1944)</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 1925 a constituent assembly abolished the Principality of Albania and proclaimed a republic with <b>Tirana</b> as capital, electing Zogu president for seven years.</blockquote><a href="/state/albanian-republic">Albanian Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The Congress of Lushnjë in 1920 created a regency council, moved the capital to <b>Tirana</b> and secured League of Nations membership.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-albania">Principality of Albania</a></li><li><blockquote>Essad ruled central Albania, from Durrës to around <b>Tirana</b> and Elbasan, through a senate of local notables and the support of Muslim landowners and his own armed retainers.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-central-albania">Republic of Central Albania</a></li></ul></section>
